{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01FWXNCvjzDUxQvyxEHZKAmD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The cognitive level classifier classifies \"Calculate the total voltage of three cells in series\" as \"understand\" instead of \"apply\". The issue is that \"calcul\" should match the `apply` pattern. Let me check the pattern:\n\n```python\n(\"apply\", r\"\\b(calculat|compute|use|apply|demonstrat|implement|perform|show)\\b\"),\n```\n\nThe text is \"Calculate the total voltage of three cells in series\". The pattern is looking for `calculat` (lowercase), but the classify function does `.lower()` first... Let me check:\n\n```python\ndef classify_cognitive(text: str) -> str:\n\n\n```\n\nAh, I see the issue now. When the text gets lowercased to \"calculate the total voltage\", the regex pattern `\\b(calculat|...)` won't match because `calculat` is just a stem—there's no word boundary after it within the word \"calculate\", so the pattern fails to trigger the `apply` classification.\n\nI need to rethink the regex approach. Instead of using word boundaries at both ends, I should either drop the trailing `\\b` or use a lookahead to allow word characters to follow the stem. Let me adjust the COGNITIVE_PATTERNS to handle partial word matches properly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":584,"cache_read_input_tokens":47089,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":584},"output_tokens":29,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39c56169-26c0-472a-94b4-c164c63a4f53","uuid":"09bc8448-b5b5-45dd-ac53-3a5151a0b209"}
